Attack Surface Management
Frequently Asked Questions
December 2025
This FAQ document is designed to help customers understand what Wiz’s Attack Surface Management
services (ASM Services) are and why some ASM Features require additional legal terms.
What are ASM Services?
Wiz’s ASM Services validate the exposure level of ports and IP addresses in your environment that are
potentially accessible from the public internet to determine their level of exposure and remove false
positives.
For example, Wiz’s ASM Services may: (i) test whether a resource is accessible from the public internet
(ii) test authentication methods on a customer’s resource log-in page by using default or weak
credentials to identify security issues (iii) validate the exploitability of vulnerabilities or
misconfigurations in a customer’s resource using various techniques. More information about Wiz’s ASM
Services can be found in the Wiz Documentation here.
Wiz's ASM Services also allow the customer to provide custom domain names or IP addresses for
scanning.
Why would I need Wiz to scan additional IP addresses or domain names?
Customers may utilize this feature to scan resources that Wiz Cloud offering doesn't scan, such as on
premises resources that are potentially open to the public internet.
What are potential changes or disruptions to the customer’s resources that could occur by using
ASM Services?
The ASM Services may add records, files or users to the customer’s resource in order to validate a
potential vulnerability. Such records, files or users will be created with strong passwords and named in
a way that clearly indicates that they were created by Wiz. In rare cases, customers may experience
unplanned downtime if the ASM Services exploitability validation process is identified by other security
tools as a threat, resulting in such security tool shutting down access to the resource being scanned,
or due to numerous concurrent requests.
Why does Wiz require these additional Wiz Attack Surface Management Terms for some ASM
Features if the Customer already has a WSA in place with Wiz?
Some ASM Features allow the Customer to have complete decision and control over which domains or
IP addresses the ASM Services scan. In some cases, unauthorized scanning of third-party resources
could be violative of laws regulating the unauthorized access to computers. In order to ensure that the
ASM Services do not violate such laws, Wiz has put in place these additional terms to ensure that Wiz
customers obtain proper consent and authorization from third parties prior to using the ASM Services
on any third party’s domain or IP address.
